comments.body:
PR review: The per-instantiation isolation of the P scratch (the thread_local bf16 buffer the PV kernel multiplies) protects nothing that a caller can observe, so the comment and the PR body item "Is the kv_mul-4 path unchanged?" (kv_mul = query heads per KV head) lean on a fact with no consequence [fwiw].

Evidence, lines 279-282:
```
//   the padded tile rows contribute nothing. A function template's static
//   locals exist once per specialization. This scratch therefore belongs to
//   the 4-head or the 16-head instantiation only, and a 4-head call never
//   sees a 16-head call's rows.
```
In TDPBF16PS output row i comes only from row i of the A operand. Note [Tile stores write 16 rows] says so [h/tron/kernels/amx_attn_iface.hpp:103-105]. A shared scratch would change only rows 4..15 of `o`, and every caller ignores those rows [h/tron/models/self_attention.hpp:1795-1800]. The `= {}` zero init still matters: it keeps the tile load from reading uninitialized memory.

Reading cost: a reviewer following the PR body looks for a corruption hazard that does not exist.

Proposed replacement for lines 276-283:
```
// P: 16 rows x 64 bf16 bit patterns (the AVX-512 convert instruction writes
// raw 16-bit lanes, so this scratch stays uint16_t). Rows group_heads..15 are
// zero padding (never written after thread init); they produce rows
// group_heads..15 of `o`, which every caller ignores (Note [Tile stores write
// 16 rows]). Each instantiation has its own copy of this static, so the
// 4-head copy's padding rows stay zero even after a 16-head call on the same
// thread.
```
In the PR body, drop the "thread_local P scratch is one per instantiation" bullet, or restate it as "the per-instantiation P scratch only affects rows of `o` that callers ignore".

PR review: The PR body names the stage-3 and stage-4 shuffle "vshufps", but the code uses `_mm512_shuffle_f32x4`, which is VSHUFF32X4, a different instruction with a different selector meaning [nit].

Evidence, lines 178-181:
```
__m512 p = _mm512_shuffle_f32x4(u[0][k], u[1][k], pair_sel);
__m512 q2 = _mm512_shuffle_f32x4(u[2][k], u[3][k], pair_sel);
...
    s_rows + k * s_stride_floats, _mm512_shuffle_f32x4(p, q2, quad_sel));
```
VSHUFF32X4 moves whole 128-bit lanes. VSHUFPS (`_mm512_shuffle_ps`) moves 32-bit elements inside each 128-bit lane. Under VSHUFPS the same immediates 0x88 and 0xdd select even and odd 32-bit elements inside each lane, not even and odd 128-bit lanes. A reviewer who follows question 1 with the vshufps table cannot reproduce the "lane `l` becomes row `4*l + k`" argument, which needs whole lanes to move. The code comment at lines 154-157 already names `_mm512_shuffle_f32x4`, so only the PR body needs the change.

Proposed fix in the PR body (section "How to review this PR", question 1, first bullet): replace "the `vshufps` selector" with "the `_mm512_shuffle_f32x4` (VSHUFF32X4, 128-bit lane shuffle) selector".

PR review: The Verification table says the kill-switch run skipped "2 sections", but the skip happens at the test-case level, so 2 test cases (holding 4 Catch2 sections) are skipped [nit].

Evidence, lines 316-318 (the same pattern repeats at lines 330-333 for the PV check):
```
if (!tron::amx_attn_h128g4::available()) {
  WARN("AMX unavailable on this host - QK precision check skipped");
  return;
```
The return leaves the test case before Catch2 enters either of its two sections ("4 heads: ..." at line 320 and "16 heads: ..." at line 323, and the pair at lines 334 and 337).

Reading cost: a reader who counts sections in the file (two per case) cannot match the number 2.

Proposed replacement for the table cell (row "AMX off (kill switch)"): "2 test cases (the QK and PV precision checks) skipped with 'AMX unavailable on this host'". Keep "4 pass" unchanged. Catch2 WARN leaves both skipped cases in the passed count.

PR review: Two stacked header comments sit above all five using-declarations, so the line about "the two group sizes" also reads as describing HEAD_SIZE_128, PAGE_TOKENS_64 and TILE_ROWS_16 [nit].

Evidence, lines 36-42:
```
// The kernel shape, by the names the interface header gives it.
// The two group sizes the kernels serve (query heads per KV head).
using tron::amx_attn_h128g4::GROUP_HEADS_16;
using tron::amx_attn_h128g4::GROUP_HEADS_4;
using tron::amx_attn_h128g4::HEAD_SIZE_128;
using tron::amx_attn_h128g4::PAGE_TOKENS_64;
