```
On main the group-size remark was a trailing comment on the single GROUP_HEADS_4 line. The second heading is wrong for the last three names.

Reading cost: the reader has to work out which comment covers which lines. clang-format sorts consecutive using-declarations ("GROUP_HEADS_16" < "GROUP_HEADS_4" < "HEAD_SIZE_128"), so the fix is to name the two constants in the sentence rather than to move the comment or reorder the lines.

Proposed replacement for lines 36-37:
```
// The kernel shape, by the names the interface header gives it.
// GROUP_HEADS_4 and GROUP_HEADS_16 are the two group sizes (query heads per
// KV head) the kernels serve.
```

PR review: The ROWS_PER_LANE_4 comment repeats the lane-to-column mapping of lines 152-153 and the column-to-output-row mapping of lines 154-155 in the same comment block [nit].

Evidence, lines 165-167:
```
// A 128-bit lane holds 4 fp32 values: lane `lane` of u[.][0..3] holds tile
// columns 4 * lane .. 4 * lane + 3, which become 4 consecutive output rows.
constexpr size_t ROWS_PER_LANE_4 = 4;
```
Lines 152-153 already say "128-bit lane `lane` (0..3) of u[row_group][k] holds tile column 4 * lane + k". Lines 154-155 already say "Output row 4 * lane + k ... is assembled from lane `lane` of u[0..3][k]". The only new fact in lines 165-166 is that a 128-bit lane holds 4 fp32 values.

Reading cost: the reader compares the two statements to find a difference and finds none.

Proposed replacement for lines 165-166:
```
// A 128-bit lane holds 4 fp32 values, so one lane yields 4 output rows.
```

