ben-review: The unpacked score tile is described by two different expressions, `__m512 u[4][4]` at its definition and `__m512 (&u)[TILE_ROWS_16 / ROWS_PER_LANE_4][ROWS_PER_LANE_4]` at the new helper's parameter, so one local alias would define that shape once [nit].

Evidence (HEAD):
```
171     const __m512 (&u)[TILE_ROWS_16 / ROWS_PER_LANE_4][ROWS_PER_LANE_4],
233     __m512 u[4][4];
255     store_transposed_rows<0>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);
```
The helper and its parameter type are new in this PR. Line 233 is pre-existing.

The concept is the 16 x 16 score tile after the two unpack stages, held as 4 row groups x 4 registers. Reading cost: at line 255 the reader must evaluate `TILE_ROWS_16 / ROWS_PER_LANE_4` to 4 to see that the reference binds to `u`. The compiler enforces the match, so this is a reading cost, not a defect.

Proposed fix, in the anonymous namespace next to the helper:
```cpp
// The 16 x 16 score tile after the two unpack stages: 4 row groups x 4
// registers, one register per output row of the row group.
using unpacked_tile = __m512[TILE_ROWS_16 / ROWS_PER_LANE_4][ROWS_PER_LANE_4];

template <int lane>
inline void store_transposed_rows(
    const unpacked_tile& u, float* s_block, size_t s_stride_floats) {
```
and at line 233 `unpacked_tile u;`. The `< 4` loop bounds at lines 231 and 236 are untouched by the diff and can stay.

ben-review: The lanes to store are chosen by testing one group size by name instead of deriving the lane count from `group_heads`, so a third accepted group size would leave rows 4..group_heads-1 unwritten with no compile-time signal [nit].

Evidence (HEAD lines 255-260):
```
    store_transposed_rows<0>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);
    if constexpr (group_heads == GROUP_HEADS_16) {
      store_transposed_rows<1>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);
      store_transposed_rows<2>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);
      store_transposed_rows<3>(u, s_block, s_stride_floats);
    }
